Overlay patch 0034 - the screen framebuffer stops LOADING a depth buffer
nobody wrote.

CLASS: (b) upstream bug fix (JeodC/libultraship, and the same three lines are in
Kenix3/libultraship). Not iOS-gated - the macOS oracle must render identically.

`SetupScreenFramebuffer` builds framebuffer 0's render pass descriptor from
scratch every frame, because the colour attachment IS the drawable's texture and
the drawable rotates. The depth attachment is a private texture that is kept
across frames, and its load action is `LoadActionLoad`.

There is nothing to load. Nothing outside the frame writes that depth texture,
and the contents that survive are from a DIFFERENT drawable's frame - the screen
pass is the final composite plus ImGui, which is drawn with depth testing off
(and `SetupFloatingFrame` detaches the depth attachment for ImGui outright). So
the load is a full read of the attachment for data that is meaningless when it
arrives: 2736x1260 x 4 B = 13.8 MB per frame at the iPhone Air's native drawable,
1.7 GB/s of pure waste at 120 Hz.

`LoadActionClear` (the descriptor already sets `clearDepth(1)`, which was dead
code until now) reads nothing at all: on a TBDR GPU a clear is resolved in tile
memory. Behaviour is strictly more defined than before, not less - a depth test
on the screen pass now sees a cleared buffer instead of whatever the previous
drawable left.

THE COMPANION RULE, AND THE BUG IT PREVENTS. Framebuffer 0 is not always just
the composite: at SSAA 1.0 with MSAA off and no post passes, `mRendersToFb` is
false (`interpreter.cpp:7126`) and PM64 draws depth-tested scene geometry
straight into it. A mid-frame render-pass restart there must therefore LOAD the
depth this descriptor now says to Clear. Overlay 0033 rev 2 owns that rule: a
deferred RESTART forces `LoadActionLoad` on colour and depth, only the frame's
FIRST pass uses the descriptor as it stands. Reviewing 0.0.0.11 caught this
before a device did; without it, every depth-tested draw after the per-frame
`gDPCopyFB` tested against far at SSAA 1.0 and nowhere else.

The STORE action is deliberately left alone. `DontCare` there would save the
write as well, but `CopyFramebuffer` / `SelectTextureFb` / `PbEnsureEncoder`
(overlay 0033) all re-open a pass with `LoadActionLoad` on depth, so a
`DontCare`d store would hand a mid-frame restart undefined depth. Doing that
safely means per-encoder `StoreActionUnknown` plumbing at every restart site,
which is a bigger change than this round's measurement justifies - recorded in
the design notes D29 as the next step if depth bandwidth ever shows up in a profile.

Measured motivation: the design notes D28 §2 / D29, and the user's device read of
`gpu_game_ms p50 8.60..14.50` at 2x supersampling.

--- a/external/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_metal.cpp
+++ b/external/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_metal.cpp
@@ -917,7 +917,14 @@
     }
 
     render_pass_descriptor->depthAttachment()->setTexture(fb.mDepthTexture);
-    render_pass_descriptor->depthAttachment()->setLoadAction(MTL::LoadActionLoad);
+    // PAPERBOAT (overlay 0034): CLEAR, not Load. The colour attachment of this
+    // pass is the drawable, which rotates; the depth texture persists, so a Load
+    // here reads back a different frame's depth - 13.8 MB at a 2736x1260
+    // drawable, every frame, for data nothing can use. The screen pass is the
+    // final composite plus ImGui and does not depth-test against it (and
+    // SetupFloatingFrame detaches depth for ImGui entirely). clearDepth(1) below
+    // was already set and until now was dead.
+    render_pass_descriptor->depthAttachment()->setLoadAction(MTL::LoadActionClear);
     render_pass_descriptor->depthAttachment()->setStoreAction(MTL::StoreActionStore);
     render_pass_descriptor->depthAttachment()->setClearDepth(1);
 
